Wrote confusion_matrix.png and training_curve.png.

**Is the model biased toward one class?** It predicts Accepted for 0.4223 of
the test fold against a true rate of 0.4554, so it leans rejected by 0.0331.
Precision 0.8156 against recall 0.7562 shows the same tilt from the other
side: the two differ by 0.0593, so the errors are not symmetric and the
model is not simply guessing the majority class.

**Is it meaningfully better than random?** Yes. Accuracy 0.8111 against a
majority-class baseline of 0.5446 is a lift of +0.2665. A coin flip would
sit at 0.5000, and always answering Rejected would sit at the baseline, so
the model is extracting real signal rather than exploiting the class
imbalance.

**Is it stronger than the Module 12 two-layer network?** On the
like-for-like Masters/PhD slice this model reaches 0.8102 against Module
12's 0.7043 on 24,326 rows, a difference of +0.1059. It gets there with
66,955,010 parameters against 49, which is roughly 1,366,428 times the
capacity for what is a modest change in accuracy - the honest reading is
that most of the available signal in this dataset was already reachable from
six structured features.

**Do the comments help?** Blanking the comments field at inference time,
with no retraining, moves accuracy from 0.8111 to 0.7265. Keeping the
comments is therefore worth +0.0846 accuracy, so the free text is carrying
real signal. That is the clearest available measure of what the free text
contributes over the structured fields alone.

**Is the dataset sufficient for a realistic admissions predictor?** No.
Every input is self-reported by whoever chose to post, GRE scores are
present for well under a tenth of rows, and nothing in the data records
letters of recommendation, statement quality, research fit, funding lines,
or how many seats a program had that year. The model can only learn the
shape of what applicants volunteer, which is a different thing from how
admissions committees decide.

